  "response": "The pivotal principle in disputes about intention is the doctrine of double effect. That the doctrine distinguishes effects that are intended from effects that are merely foreseen shows that it permits the causing of a foreseen harm that is not intended. The doctrine also permits a harmful effect to be intended as a means to a good end, and a licence of that breadth cannot be squared with a permission confined to harms that fall outside what the agent intends. Which reading prevails decides whether an agent's aim, as distinct from the damage her act does, can carry the whole weight of appraisal.\n\nThe provenance of the principle, and of the examples used to test it, is instructive in its own right. Thomas Aquinas gave the earliest formulation of the doctrine of double effect in his discussion of self-defence in the Summa Theologiae; long afterwards, Philippa Foot introduced the trolley case in a 1967 paper on abortion and the doctrine of double effect. Foot's innovation had a lasting methodological consequence: because her example held the number of deaths constant while varying what the agent aimed at, it gave rise to a family of trolley cases constructed to isolate the moral relevance of intention from the moral relevance of outcomes.\n\nThe difficulty of settling such a dispute in a concrete case is well illustrated by the English decision in Re A, which concerned conjoined twins given the names Jodie and Mary. Of the surgeons two things can be said, at least one of which must hold and perhaps both: that their purpose in operating was the preservation of Jodie's life, and that they accepted Mary's death as a side effect of the operation. Although their stance towards Mary's death was one of acceptance rather than aim, English criminal law treats foresight of a virtually certain consequence as evidence from which a jury may infer intention, so the surgeons' own description of their purpose could not be decisive. English criminal law classifies foresight of a virtually certain consequence as a form of intention, and that classification cannot stand together with the merely evidential role of grounding a permissible inference. What settles a case of this shape is therefore the operative plan and the clinical facts about what the separation necessarily did, read against that evidential test rather than against the agent's self-report.\n\nDisputes of this kind arise because the leading theoretical options locate moral status in different places. Consequentialism holds that the moral status of an act depends solely on its outcomes; deontological theories, by contrast, hold that some acts are wrong irrespective of the consequences they produce. Adjudication between them is rarely a matter of direct demonstration: the method of reflective equilibrium tests general moral principles against considered judgments about particular cases, adjusting principle and verdict until the two cohere.",
